  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "In response to ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://article.gmane.org/gmane.comp.cms.fedora-commons.devel/1066"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://article.gmane.org/gmane.comp.cms.fedora-commons.devel/1066",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "Bill wrote:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "> It occurs to me with this request that the states we use currently use for",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> objects in Fedora are primarily for user convenience. Is there anything",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> stopping us from allowing a repository admin to define the states in which",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> an object can exist? This is a long way from being workflow, but I could see",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> it facilitating the creation of a simple customized workflow on top of",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "> Fedora, especially with the ability to do access control based on state.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
